[Round "7.9"]
[White "6. Typical Mistakes"]
[Black "Puzzle #6"]
[Result "*"]
[FEN "8/6pk/7p/1P3p2/2qBpP2/3bP2P/2p3PK/2Q5 b - - 0 1"]

1... { -KEY- } Bf1 { You might have considered the plan of somehow bringing your Queen to b1. However, using schematic thinking, we realise that White can simply put his Bishop on b2 and stop the c-pawn from queening forever. Instead, we should go after g2. Don't worry if you did not even consider a King hunt. The motif of a vulnerable King was not obvious at all. } 2. Kg1 Qe2 { White is overwhelmed with the two weaknesses, his g2 square and Black's threat of queening the c-pawn. } 3. Qxf1 Qxf1+ 4. Kxf1 c1=Q+ { After using PIE on this pattern, I hope that you can see this position under different lenses. Specifically, how White's King has no real defenders. White is effectively a Queen down because she is keeping watch of the c1 square. As for White's dark-squared Bishop, it was no help in defending Black's light square assault on g2. } 
 *

